WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.072
On this slide, we are focusing on compare values.

00:00:03.072 --> 00:00:07.792
Start with the meaning of the idea before worrying about the exact syntax.

00:00:07.792 --> 00:00:15.567
Ask what information is going in, what Python is doing with it, and what result should come out.

00:00:15.567 --> 00:00:18.642
Before clicking Run, predict the output.

00:00:18.642 --> 00:00:23.217
Then run the cell, inspect the result, and change one value or condition at a time.

00:00:23.217 --> 00:00:26.562
Rerun it and explain why the output changed.

00:00:26.562 --> 00:00:33.257
The goal is to understand the relationship between input, process, and output.

00:00:33.257 --> 00:00:38.692
In real analytics, this pattern can support thresholds, validation rules, category assignment, alerts,

00:00:38.692 --> 00:00:41.722
and repeated processing.

00:00:41.722 --> 00:00:47.514
The examples are intentionally small so you can trace the logic from the condition to the result.
